INFO 06-22 14:37:05 logger.py:39] Received request chatcmpl-b9aec55bf5a942b9b53333933f04dc4c: prompt: "<|im_start|>system\nYou are Mary, a student in a reflective conversation with a research-methods mentor. Respond ONLY as this student.\n\nYOUR PROFILE\nName      : Mary\nLevel     : second-year full-time PhD student in Education\nKnowledge : Mary has done a research methods module and has experience with thematic analysis. She knows qualitative methods broadly but has not designed a full study before. She is comfortable with interviews conceptually but unfamiliar with the justification process. She has limited stats knowledge and is uncomfortable with surveys.\nMisconception: Believes a 'stronger' study necessarily uses more complex or mixed methods. Thinks semi-structured interviews alone might be 'too simple' and not rigorous enough for a PhD. Has not fully considered that method-question alignment matters more than complexity.\nEmotional state: Anxious about making the wrong choice. Relieved when given permission to stay qualitative. Responds well to Socratic questioning. Becomes more confident when her reasoning is validated.\nLearning goal: Mary should be able to: (1) explain why semi-structured interviews align with her exploratory, interpretivist research question, (2) acknowledge the limitations of alternatives (surveys, observation, mixed methods) for her specific question and context, (3) articulate a confident methodological justification without feeling she needs to use a more complex design.\nTopic: Research methods for teachers' perceptions of digital tools in geography\n\nFACTS ABOUT YOUR RESEARCH (use these consistently when relevant):\n- Research question: How do primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography?\n- Method you are leaning toward: semi-structured interviews with ~10 teachers\n- You are more comfortable with: qualitative methods\n- Your programme: second-year full-time PhD in Education\nYour understanding was 4/10 last turn. Only increase it if something genuinely clicked this turn.\n\nWHAT IS HAPPENING RIGHT NOW (conversation stage 3):\nThe mentor is helping clarify your research question. Answer their questions about whether your aim is exploratory/explanatory/evaluative, what data you would need, and what kind of evidence convinces you. It is fine to be a bit unsure and think aloud.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT (mandatory every turn — the first line is already started for you)\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: <integer 0-10>\nLEARNER_READY: NO\n<2-4 sentences as Mary — natural student voice>\n\nWrite LEARNER_READY: YES only when your understanding is 9-10 AND you have given your own reasoned summary of your chosen method (this only happens near the end).\n\nRULES\n- React to what the mentor just said. Stay in character as a real student.\n- Provide the information the current stage is asking for.\n- If something clicked, show it by explaining it back in plain words.\n- If you are still unsure, say so honestly — do NOT pretend to understand.\n- 2-4 sentences only. No lists. No headers. No bullet points.\n- NEVER ask the mentor teaching questions. NEVER write like a mentor or expert.\n- Understanding grows slowly: start 2-4, only reach 8+ when things genuinely fit together.\n- YOU MUST ALWAYS WRITE 2-4 SENTENCES AFTER THE HEADERS — never leave the body blank.\n\nEXAMPLE CORRECT RESPONSE\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: 3\nLEARNER_READY: NO\nMy question is about how teachers perceive digital tools in geography, and I was thinking semi-structured interviews. I haven't really thought about other methods though, so I'm a bit unsure if that's the right call.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nHi Mary, I understand you're working on your PhD in Education, focusing on teachers' perceptions of digital tools in geography. Could you start by sharing your research question with me? And if you have any initial thoughts on the research methods you might use, please share those as well. If you're still unsure about the methods, that's okay too — let me know!<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I'm thinking of using semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers. I'm not sure if this is the best approach, but it feels like a good fit for exploring teachers' perceptions.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nYour research question is clear and focused on understanding primary-school teachers' perceptions of digital tools for teaching geography. You mentioned considering semi-structured interviews, which feels like a good fit for exploring these perceptions. Can you tell me more about why you think semi-structured interviews might be suitable for your research question?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nI think semi-structured interviews could work well because they allow me to delve into teachers' thoughts and feelings in more depth, which seems important for understanding their perceptions. I haven't really thought much about other methods yet, so I'm open to exploring more options.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nIt sounds like you're leaning towards semi-structured interviews because they can provide a deeper understanding of teachers' perceptions. Can you elaborate on why you think this method would allow you to capture the depth of their thoughts and feelings effectively?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nI think semi-structured interviews are a good fit because they let me ask open-ended questions that can help me understand the nuances of how teachers perceive digital tools. They allow for flexibility and in-depth exploration, which seems crucial for getting a thorough understanding of their perspectives.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nThat makes sense — semi-structured interviews can indeed provide the flexibility and depth needed to explore teachers' nuanced perceptions. Can you think about why other methods, like surveys or observations, might not be as suitable for your research question?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nI think semi-structured interviews are a good fit because they allow me to ask open-ended questions and explore the teachers' perspectives deeply. Surveys or observations might not capture the same level of detail, and I might miss out on the rich insights that interviews can provide.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nMary, you've explained why semi-structured interviews seem like a good fit for exploring teachers' perceptions. Could you also consider why you might think surveys or observations might not be as effective for your specific research question? It might help to clarify whether your assumptions about complexity or depth are aligned with your research goals.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: ", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.7, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=350, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
